<Layout title="Cannabis History" description="5,000 years of cannabis history — from ancient medicine to modern prohibition and back again. A complete historical timeline.">
  <main class="history-page">

    <!-- Hero Banner -->
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-bg"></div>
      <div class="hero-content">
        <div class="hero-icon">📖</div>
        <h1>5,000 Years of Cannabis History</h1>
        <p class="hero-subtitle">From ancient medicine to modern prohibition — and back again</p>
        <div class="hero-stats">
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-num">10,000+</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Years of Human Use</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-divider"></div>
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-num">26</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Key Historical Events</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-divider"></div>
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-num">6</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Civilizational Eras</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-scroll">
        <span>Scroll to explore</span>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M7 13l5 5 5-5M7 7l5 5 5-5"/></svg>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Timeline -->
    <section class="timeline-section">
      <div class="container">

        {timelineEras.map((era, eraIdx) => (
          <div class="era-block">
            <div class="era-header" style={`--era-accent: ${era.accent}`}>
              <div class="era-header-inner">
                <span class="era-number">{String(eraIdx + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <div>
                  <h2 class="era-name">{era.era}</h2>
                  <span class="era-period">{era.period}</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="era-line" style={`background: ${era.accent}`}></div>
            </div>

            <div class="events-list">
              {era.events.map((event, evIdx) => (
                <div class={`event-card ${evIdx % 2 === 0 ? 'left' : 'right'}`} data-badge={event.badge}>
                  <div class="event-connector">
                    <div class="connector-dot" style={`background: ${era.accent}; box-shadow: 0 0 12px ${era.accent}80`}></div>
                    <div class="connector-line" style={`background: linear-gradient(180deg, ${era.accent}60, transparent)`}></div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="event-content" style={`--card-accent: ${era.accent}`}>
                    <div class="event-year" style={`color: ${era.accent}`}>{event.year}</div>
                    <div class="event-location">{event.location}</div>
                    <h3 class="event-title">{event.title}</h3>
                    <p class="event-description">{event.description}</p>
                    <span class="badge" style={`background: ${event.badgeColor}22; color: ${event.badgeColor}; border-color: ${event.badgeColor}44`}>
                      {event.badge}
                    </span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            {eraIdx < timelineEras.length - 1 && (
              <div class="era-divider">
                <div class="divider-line"></div>
                <div class="divider-icon">🌿</div>
                <div class="divider-line"></div>
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Propaganda Callout -->
    <section class="propaganda-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="propaganda-box">
          <div class="propaganda-header">
            <span class="propaganda-icon">⚠️</span>
            <h2>The Prohibition Propaganda Machine</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="propaganda-content">
            <div class="propaganda-main">
              <h3>How a Plant Was Criminalized Through Lies, Racism, and Greed</h3>
              <p>The prohibition of cannabis was not a scientific or medical decision — it was a political one, built on a foundation of racial fear, yellow journalism, and industrial self-interest. Understanding this history is essential to understanding why prohibition persisted for so long despite overwhelming evidence of cannabis's safety and medical value.</p>

              <div class="propaganda-cards">
                <div class="prop-card">
                  <h4>🎬 Reefer Madness (1936)</h4>
                  <p>The 1936 propaganda film "Reefer Madness" (originally "Tell Your Children") depicted cannabis users descending into manslaughter, suicide, and rape after a single use. Financed by a church group and distributed by exploitation filmmaker Dwain Esper, the film was so absurdly melodramatic that it was rediscovered in the 1970s as a camp classic. But in 1936, it was presented as a factual documentary to terrified parents and legislators. It represents the low point of cannabis misinformation — a brazen attempt to create fear through fiction presented as fact.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="prop-card">
                  <h4>📰 Hearst Newspaper Campaign</h4>
                  <p>William Randolph Hearst, the newspaper magnate, ran a relentless anti-cannabis campaign across his nationwide newspaper chain throughout the 1930s. Historians have pointed to two likely motivations: first, Hearst had invested heavily in timber for paper production and faced economic competition from hemp paper; second, he harbored known racist attitudes and saw the "marijuana menace" as a useful tool for inflaming anti-immigrant sentiment. His papers routinely ran fabricated stories attributing violent crimes to cannabis — stories that Harry Anslinger cited as evidence in congressional hearings, creating a circular loop of manufactured panic.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="prop-card">
                  <h4>💬 Harry Anslinger's Racist Quotes</h4>
                  <p>Harry Anslinger, who served as the first commissioner of the Federal Bureau of Narcotics from 1930 to 1962, was the primary architect of American cannabis prohibition — and he achieved it through explicit racism. Among his documented statements: "There are 100,000 total marijuana smokers in the US, and most are Negroes, Hispanics, Filipinos, and entertainers. Their Satanic music, jazz and swing result from marijuana use. This marijuana causes white women to seek sexual relations with Negroes." These quotes appeared in his official communications and congressional testimony. They are not outliers — they represent the actual intellectual foundation of American drug prohibition.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="prop-card">
                  <h4>🏭 Industrial Motives: Hemp vs. Nylon</h4>
                  <p>DuPont Chemical patented nylon in 1937 — the same year the Marihuana Tax Act passed. DuPont's financial backer, banker Andrew Mellon (who was also Harry Anslinger's uncle-in-law), served as Secretary of the Treasury and oversaw the Bureau of Narcotics. Hemp fiber, which had been economically dominant for centuries, was suddenly a direct competitor to synthetics that DuPont and other petrochemical companies were investing billions to develop. The simultaneous criminalization of hemp and the launch of synthetic fibers is a historical coincidence that many economists and historians find insufficiently coincidental.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Religion Section -->
    <section class="culture-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Cannabis in Global Religions & Cultures</h2>
          <p>A sacred plant across six of the world's major religious and spiritual traditions</p>
        </div>
        <div class="religion-grid">
          {religions.map(r => (
            <div class="religion-card">
              <div class="religion-icon">{r.icon}</div>
              <div class="religion-name">{r.name}</div>
              <div class="religion-tradition">{r.tradition}</div>
              <p class="religion-desc">{r.description}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Famous Users Section -->
    <section class="famous-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Famous Historical Cannabis Users</h2>
          <p>Kings, presidents, philosophers, and artists — cannabis's company through history</p>
        </div>
        <div class="famous-grid">
          {famousUsers.map(u => (
            <div class="famous-card">
              <div class="famous-header">
                <div class="famous-icon">{u.icon}</div>
                <div>
                  <div class="famous-name">{u.name}</div>
                  <div class="famous-years">{u.years}</div>
                </div>
                {u.verified && <span class="verified-badge">✓ Documented</span>}
              </div>
              <div class="famous-claim">{u.claim}</div>
              <p class="famous-detail">{u.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Footer Callout -->
    <section class="footer-cta">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="cta-box">
          <h2>10,000 Years of History. Still Unfolding.</h2>
          <p>From the steppes of Central Asia to the dispensaries of Colorado, cannabis has been humanity's most enduring botanical companion — medicine, sacrament, fiber, and symbol of liberation. The story is still being written.</p>
          <div class="cta-links">
            <a href={`${base}strains`} class="cta-btn primary">Explore Strains →</a>
            <a href={`${base}map`} class="cta-btn secondary">Legal Map →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</Layout>

